  "answer": "Low uridine salvage sensitizes cytarabine-residual AML to teriflunomide-induced differentiation\n\nHypothesis and premises. In cytarabine-residual AML with persistent differentiation blockade, insufficient adaptive uridine-salvage capacity relative to contemporaneous pyrimidine demand permits human-compatible teriflunomide exposure to induce DHODH-dependent, durable maturation and reduce leukemia-propagating frequency. Normal-cell sparing is conditional on adequate salvage reserve, not presumed. DHODH inhibition promoted experimental AML differentiation [S4]; studied cytarabine-residual cells displayed oxidative features without consistent stem-cell enrichment [S2]. Analyze these separately from the ROS-low, BCL-2-associated stem-cell population studied in [S1]. Oxidative metabolism alone is not the proposed sensitivity determinant. Venetoclax–azacitidine is an established comparator [S3], not the repurposing proposal.\n\nDrug and exposure gaps. Unverified prior knowledge requiring confirmation: teriflunomide is approved for relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis, inhibits DHODH, and transporter/UCK-mediated uridine salvage bypasses de novo pyrimidine synthesis. The packet supplies no verifying drug-specific evidence. Require regulatory-label, biochemical, and clinical pharmacokinetic/binding verification before efficacy testing. Establish a numerical licensed-dose unbound concentration–time envelope, including peak, trough, AUC and duration. Measure dilution-controlled marrow/plasma uridine and reproduce its observed range, including the upper end, in protein-containing stromal cocultures. Directly measure free drug in cultures and xenografts; do not escalate above the envelope. Human-compatible engagement, differentiation and therapeutic selectivity remain speculative bridges.\n\nAdaptive biomarker. Acute incorporation alone cannot establish salvage capacity, and pretreatment demand may change. In parallel aliquots, measure isotope-resolved salvage flux under calibrated reference-DHODH blockade across measured marrow-uridine concentrations and early/adaptive intervals before maturation. Validate reserve estimates using nucleotide-pool mass balance and transporter/UCK perturbations. Estimate C(t), maximal sustainable salvage flux at each uridine concentration, and D(t), contemporaneous demand from macromolecular incorporation, irreversible losses and pool balance, in matching per-cell-time units. Sustained R(t)=C(t)/D(t)<1 is provisional; validate independently and analyze R continuously. After residual selection, inducibly increase or decrease verified limiting transport/UCK activity. Require reciprocal sensitivity shifts at matched free exposure and engagement, accounting for changing demand and drug-free growth effects.\n\nNovelty boundary. DHODH-driven differentiation is established [S4]; salvage bypass alone is not a discovery. Prior teriflunomide/leflunomide–AML and salvage-resistance literature requires checking. The proposed increment is response prediction by adaptive R beyond baseline uptake, UCK expression, cycling and respiration. Test this in held-out paired pretreatment/residual samples, including an R-by-residual-state interaction rather than assuming residual-specific sensitivity. Failure to improve prediction negates the biomarker advantage; drug-specific novelty remains unresolved.\n\nDiscriminating tests. Include vehicle, a characterized DHODH inhibitor, genetic DHODH suppression, and venetoclax–azacitidine. Confirm engagement and pyrimidine depletion before maturation or apoptosis. Track index-sorted, initially immature single-cell lineages and confirm leukemic genotype in mature descendants; assess post-washout morphology/immunophenotyping separately from killing, viable yield and colonies. Randomized, blinded xenograft studies should quantify secondary-transplant limiting-dilution frequency after verified washout with equal viable malignant-cell doses across groups, reporting total killing separately. Uridine rescue initiated simultaneously with drug must isotope-confirm prevention of depletion without changing exposure or engagement. Supraphysiological rescue tests mechanism, not physiological protection. Delayed rescue tests reversibility: persistent maturation after commitment does not falsify pyrimidine-dependent initiation.\n\nSelectivity gap. Normal-cell toxicity assays alone cannot establish salvage-mediated selectivity. Quantify adaptive R, cycling and DHODH engagement in normal CD34-positive subsets under identical free exposure. Use cycling-matched comparisons and bidirectional reserve perturbations in normal and malignant cells; measure normal colonies and engraftment. Attribute sparing to salvage only if reserve-dependent response persists after accounting for demand and engagement differences.\n\nFalsification and limitations. Off-target toxicity, cytostasis and selection of pre-existing mature cells are alternative explanations. Reproducible absence of durable lineage-proven maturation or reduced propagating frequency, despite engagement and sustained depletion at permitted exposure in validated low-R residual AML, refutes the central prediction. Response despite prevention of depletion from treatment onset contradicts the mechanism; absent reciprocal reserve effects rejects salvage adequacy as the determinant. Inadequate engagement within the envelope rejects translational feasibility, not DHODH biology. Dynamic demand, physiological uridine, AML heterogeneity, marrow pharmacokinetics and progenitor toxicity may erase the therapeutic window. Clinical benefit is unestablished.",
